## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical E2E test craft for user onboarding, focusing on external service mocks, data isolation, and idempotent behavior—key in fintech onboarding.

## Key Concepts

- E2E testing of authentication flows
- Mocking external email gateways
- Test data seeding and isolation
- Token expiry and single-use guarantees
- Deterministic assertions across browsers

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example (Cypress)
describe('Email verification onboarding', () => {
  it('activates user via one-time link and prevents reuse', () => {
    cy.request('POST', '/api/signup', { email: 'a@example.com', password: 'P@ssw0rd' })
    .then(() => {
      cy.task('getVerificationLink', 'a@example.com').then(link => {
        cy.visit(link);
        cy.get('#status').should('contain', 'Active');
        cy.visit(link); // reuse attempt
        cy.get('#error').should('exist');
      });
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate email expiry deterministically in a CI environment?
- How would you verify that signing up with the same email twice does not create duplicates?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for optimistic UI and offline resilience are common in messaging apps. This question probes ability to design deterministic E2E tests that cover front-end and back-end coordination, error paths, and data isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Optimistic UI updates
- Network fault simulation and retry handling
- Test data isolation and deterministic seeding
- Front-end/back-end state reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test snippet (simplified)
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('delete with optimistic update and Undo', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://chat.app');
  await page.fill('#newMessage', 'hello world');
  await page.click('#send');
  const msg = page.locator('.message').last();
  await expect(msg).toHaveText('hello world');

  await msg.click('.delete');
  await expect(page.locator('.toast-undo')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).not.toContainText('hello world');

  await page.click('.toast-undo');
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).toContainText('hello world');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle multiple concurrent deletions in a single channel?
- How would you verify backend state during intermittent connectivity without flaky tests?

